## Evaluator rule

- `StepEvaluator` owns `_evaluated` and `_invalid`; dense `evaluate_many` uses the vectorized `a-r0` path only when dense arrays are present, otherwise it falls back to scalar `evaluate` ([engine lines 763–824](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-c1c2suff-ws/.scratch/multi-catfish-v025-physics-successor/probe/run_v025_matrix_probe.py:763)). The dense path writes complete profiles into `_evaluated` ([lines 825–901](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-c1c2suff-ws/.scratch/multi-catfish-v025-physics-successor/probe/run_v025_matrix_probe.py:825)). Therefore assert `tape.steps[step].arrays is not None`, setting `a-r0`, and the requested boundary tuple before every batch.
- Best reusable fail-closed pattern: BASIN2 replaces the class method `StepEvaluator.evaluate` with a counter-incrementing raising stub and accesses profiles only through `_evaluated` ([BASIN2 lines 111–155](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-basin-ws/.scratch/basin2/run_basin2.py:111)). Its first comparison call explicitly contains BASE and candidates ([lines 234–255](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-basin-ws/.scratch/basin2/run_basin2.py:234)).
